Q: DataGrid catch cell value changed event with a single click on UpdateSourceTrigger = SourceUpdated A: <blockquote>
<p>To handle data changes in a WPF DataGrid when a user edits a cell, use the <code>Binding. SourceUpdated</code> attached event alongside <code>NotifyOnSourceUpdated=True</code>.This setup fires an event handler in your code-behind whenever a UI value is successfully committed back to your underlying data object</p>
</blockquote>
<p>it CAN fire when you give explicit <code>&lt;Datagrid.Columns&gt;</code> definition, like this (here it fires on TextBox of Property changed event, but can be used for checkbox as well). And you should set<code>UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged, NotifyOnSourceUpdated=True</code> for needed columns</p>
<pre><code>            &lt;DataGrid.Columns&gt;
                &lt;!-- This column will trigger the event on every keystroke --&gt;
                &lt;DataGridTextColumn
                    Header=&quot;FirstName&quot;
                    Binding=&quot;{Binding FirstName, Mode=TwoWay,  
                                UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged, NotifyOnSourceUpdated=True}&quot;/&gt;
                &lt;!-- This column will trigger the event only when the cell loses focus --&gt;
                &lt;DataGridTextColumn
                    Header=&quot;LastName&quot;
                    Binding=&quot;{Binding LastName, Mode=TwoWay,  NotifyOnSourceUpdated=True}&quot;/&gt;    
            &lt;/DataGrid.Columns&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>in datagrid-tag add method name for SourceUpdated-event handler :</p>
<pre><code>            AutoGenerateColumns=&quot;False&quot; 
            SourceUpdated=&quot;DataGrid_SourceUpdated&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>and implement event-Handler in code-behind</p>
<pre><code>    private void DataGrid_SourceUpdated(object sender, DataTransferEventArgs e)
            {
                // Handle event
                //MessageBox.Show(sender.GetType().ToString(), &quot;DataGrid_SourceUpdated&quot;);

                TextBox editedTextBox = e.TargetObject as TextBox;
               // 1. Identify the framework element that triggered the update
                if (editedTextBox != null)
                {
                    // 2. Access the data context of the row being edited
                    var updatedItem = editedTextBox.DataContext;

                    // 3. Identify the specific property that changed
                    string updatedProperty = e.Property.Name;

                    // Example Action: Trigger a database save or log changes
                    //System.Diagnostics.Debug.WriteLine(&quot;Property '{updatedProperty}' updated on item.&quot;);
                    MessageBox.Show(String.Format(&quot;Property '{0}' updated on dg item.&quot;, updatedProperty), null);
                }
            }
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>The event handler captures a DataTransferEventArgs object. You can extract the precise UI element that initiated the change and the exact properties involved. <code>e.TargetObject</code> evaluates to the underlying UI editing control (usually a TextBox or CheckBox) inside the active cell.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>P.S. Other ways:</p>
<ol>
<li>use <code>Microsoft.Xaml.Behaviors.Wpf</code></li>
<li>use <code>DatagridTemplateColumn.CellEditingTemplate</code> to apply <code>UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged</code></li>
</ol>

Q: Extra non-character or empty character or absence is getting printed at the end of target string being matched by regex pattern using find() in java A: <pre><code>import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String regex = &quot;((\\d\\d\\d\\d)(\\w\\w\\w)(\\W\\W))&quot;;
        String targetString = &quot;$()1287jga$%dp5000tag%!5000tag%!5000tag%!q1&quot;;
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(regex);
        Matcher match = pattern.matcher(targetString); 
        while(match.find()) {
            String m = match.group();
            System.out.println(m);
            System.out.println(&quot;Pattern=&gt;&quot;
              + m
              + &quot;&lt;=at starting index &quot;
              + match.start() + &quot; ending index &quot;+ match.end());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Looks like that extra index 43 was coming from the last symbol in String variable called 'regex'. that symbol was &quot;*&quot; Which I removed and now final output becomes:<br />
<code>Pattern=&gt;5000tag%!&lt;=at starting index 32 ending index 41</code><br />
Hope that is what you were aiming for.</p>
<p>Also, you did:<br />
<code>String m = match.group();</code> and you also used <strong>match.group();</strong> in the first line of 'println' method. so using that 'm' instead makes output cleaner.</p>
